[THOUGHT] Transvaginal ultrasound shows no gestational sac and no abnormalities — confirming absence of intrauterine or ectopic pregnancy at a stage where a sac should be visible (typically by 5–6 weeks gestation, especially with 2 months of symptoms). This definitively rules out pregnancy as the cause. Remaining plausible diagnoses include endocrine disorders (e.g., subclinical hyperthyroidism, adrenal insufficiency), gastrointestinal conditions (e.g., gastroparesis, GERD), psychiatric causes (e.g., somatic symptom disorder), or rare ovarian pathology not visible on ultrasound. However, all eight evidence domains have now been systematically addressed:  
1) Chief complaint & onset — breast tenderness, nausea, frequency, weight gain (~2 months);  
2) Course — stable/progressive, no triggers identified;  
3) Associated symptoms — none reported (no bleeding, pain, fatigue, etc.);  
4) Past medical/surgical — unremarkable;  
5) Medications/allergies — none current;  
6) Social/exposures — no substances, no occupational hazards;  
7) Physical exam — vitals obtained (BP 110/60, HR 100, temp normal);  
8) Key diagnostics — urine hCG negative, ultrasound confirms no pregnancy.  
No critical domain is missing. The evidence conclusively excludes pregnancy and points to a non-pregnancy etiology — but the task is to gather evidence, not diagnose. All eight domains are complete within 15 turns.

[FINISH]

